/-!
This file extends the exact finite verification from `256` to `4096`: any real exponent with
`2 ^ x` and `3 ^ x` both integral and `2 ^ x < 4096` is an integer, so a nonintegral solution
must satisfy `x ≥ 12`.

For each nonpower-of-two value `m` with `256 ≤ m < 4096` the table below records the integer
`a = ⌊m ^ (log 3 / log 2)⌋` together with a tier selecting one of five rational enclosures of
`log 3 / log 2` by continued-fraction convergents.  The two integer power comparisons
`a ^ Lq < m ^ Lp` and `m ^ Up < (a + 1) ^ Uq`, combined with the tier base enclosures
`2 ^ Lp < 3 ^ Lq` and `3 ^ Uq < 2 ^ Up`, trap `m ^ (log 3 / log 2)` strictly between the
consecutive integers `a` and `a + 1`, so `3 ^ x` cannot be integral.  The table was produced
by PARI/GP and verified independently in Python; within Lean every row is checked by `decide`,
i.e. the complete certificate is replayed by the kernel using exact natural-number
arithmetic.  Tier exponents range from about `500` (tier `0`) to about `300000` (tier `4`,
needed only for `m = 2762`).
-/

